# http — status codes, real-world reference

## The five classes

| Class | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 1xx | informational |
| 2xx | success |
| 3xx | redirection |
| 4xx | client-side error |
| 5xx | server-side error |

## 1xx Informational

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 100 | Continue — server received the headers, client may proceed |
| 101 | Switching Protocols |
| 102 | Processing |
| 103 | Early Hints |

## 2xx Success

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 200 | OK — standard response |
| 201 | Created — request fulfilled |
| 202 | Accepted |
| 203 | Non-Authoritative Information |
| 204 | No Content |
| 205 | Reset Content |

## 3xx Redirection

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 300 | Multiple Choices |
| 301 | Moved Permanently |
| 302 | Found — moved temporarily |
| 303 | See Other |

## 4xx Client errors

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 400 | Bad Request |
| 401 | Unauthorized |
| 402 | Payment Required |
| 403 | Forbidden — valid request, server refuses (permissions) |
| 404 | Not Found |

## 5xx Server errors

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 500 | Internal Server Error — generic |
| 502 | Bad Gateway |
| 503 | Service Unavailable |
| 505 | HTTP Version Not Supported |

## AWS/API Gateway notes

A 404 means communication succeeded but the resource wasn't found.
MissingSecurityHeader (S3 API call missing security info) returns 400 Bad Request.

| Code | Exception | Retryable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 400 | Bad Request Exception | No |
| 403 | Access Denied Exception | No |
| 404 | Not Found Exception | No |
| 409 | Conflict Exception | No |
| 429 | Limit Exceeded Exception | No |
| 429 | Too Many Requests Exception | Yes |
| 502 | Bad Gateway — often an incompatible Lambda proxy response | No |
| 503 | Service Unavailable Exception | Yes |
| 504 | Endpoint Request Timed-out Exception | Yes |

## Check codes from the homelab

```sh
curl -I https://example.com                                   # headers only
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://homelab:32400/web   # is Plex up?
for u in http://homelab:32400/web http://mediaserver:8096; do
  printf "%s -> " "$u"; curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" "$u"
done
```
